        body: "The geometric design follows the TTP standards, which address roadway classification, design speed, alignment, and typical sections, with tribes free to adopt standards that equal or exceed them. Access-point design covers driveway location and spacing, intersection sight distance, turn lanes where traffic volumes warrant them, and drainage so the access does not dam or divert water onto the BIA roadway. Structural section design accounts for the truck traffic the development will generate, since under-designed access pavement fails fast under construction and delivery loads.\n\nSafety analysis is an explicit regulatory topic: engineering and traffic analysis determine maximum speed limits, vehicle size and weight limits, and traffic-control devices. Where a tribal road meets a state or county highway, the state or county encroachment permit governs the connection, and its standards may control the intersection design. Construction traffic management keeps the existing road safe and passable during the work, which matters on reservations where the BIA road may be the only route serving a community. As-built documentation goes to the road owner's records so the inventory reflects what was built.",
